        "title": "CTNW",
        "content_type": "text/html;charset=utf-8",
        "text_sha256": "5706a4aa16d9bcd4a6b39be631a4790d02a7e0aae48fab54a267bf749ef0ab0f",
        "excerpt": "CTNW\nCONFERENCES\nTRAINING & WORKSHOPS\nToggle navigation\nUpcoming Events\nPast Events\nContact\nFAQ\nLog In\nSign Up\n6th Workshop on the Operation and Maintenance (O&M) of the IMS\nVienna, Austria\n11 Nov 2018 - 16 Nov 2018\nT1.1-P5 RASA RN Maintenance Experiences and Best Practices\nR.M. Hong\n1\n,\nS. Little\n1\n1\nGeneral Dynamics MS.\nAbstract:\nUnder our Global Equipment Support Contract, General Dynamics Mission Systems (GDMS) provides equipment support for the Radionuclide Aerosol Sampler/Analyzer (RASA) particulate sensor to the Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty Organization (CTBTO) and International Monitoring System (IMS) Station Operators. GDMS reviewed recent scheduled and unscheduled maintenance to RASA stations demonstrating the benefits of proper maintenance. Station infrastructure often dictates the success of a station with stable power being paramount to meeting Data Availability requirements. GDMS completed station maintenance to several sites with power issues impacting data availability. We discuss changes underway or completed to improve reliability. These include power stability studies, review of station grounding, auxiliary generator replacement and relevant monitoring technologies.\nFACEBOOK\nTWITTER\nYOUTUBE\nFLICKR",
